  <!-- Case Study -->
  <section id="case-study" style="padding: 4rem 0;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 500; color: #fff; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Case Study: EU Brand Avoiding Greenwashing Enforcement</h2>
      <p style="color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        A German sustainable fashion brand faced EU Green Claims Directive investigation in 2025. Their marketing claimed "100% sustainable production" without third-party verification. The brand sourced from a manufacturer making eco-friendly claims but lacking GOTS or OEKO-TEX certification.
      </p>
      <p style="color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        The investigation required documentation of all sustainability claims. The manufacturer could not provide valid certificate numbers or audit reports. Without verification, the brand faced potential fines of €50,000 per violation and mandatory corrective advertising.
      </p>
      <p style="color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        The brand transitioned to SDF Clothing with GOTS 7.0 certification. We provided certificate numbers verified on the official GOTS database, BSCI audit reports, and OEKO-TEX documentation. The brand updated their marketing to reflect verified certifications rather than vague claims.
      </p>
      <p style="color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        The EU investigation concluded with no penalties after the brand demonstrated verifiable certification compliance. The lesson: marketing claims without documentation create regulatory risk. Third-party verification is not optional for EU brands in 2026.
      </p>
      <p style="color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.8;">
        This case demonstrates why verification matters. Brands relying on manufacturer claims without independent audit exposure face enforcement. GOTS 7.0 certification provides the documentation regulators require for sustainability claims.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

